<section style="padding:72px 24px 40px;"><div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto;"><div class="rich"><p>A structural inspection in Perth usually starts with something you've noticed: a crack running up from a window corner, a door that suddenly sticks, a floor with a new slope or a ceiling that's started to sag. An independent structural engineer inspects the house, works out what is most likely moving and why, and writes it up. The report gives you the cause, the severity measured against the CSIRO damage categories, and the engineer's recommendations.</p><p>Kings Park Structural Co is an advertising and referral service, not an engineering firm. We pass your enquiry to an independent structural engineering firm that pays to advertise here, and that firm quotes you and contracts with you directly. Engineers quote each job, and the price depends on the house, the problem and what the report is for. Our page on <a href="/structural-engineer-cost-perth/">structural engineer costs in Perth</a> sets out what is and isn't published.</p></div></div></section>
<section style="padding:0 24px 64px;"><div style="max-width:1100px;margin:0 auto;">
    <span class="kicker">What We Handle</span>
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 28px;">What's Included in Every Visit</h2>
    <div class="feat-grid" style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:20px;">
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #ECE5D8;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Figtree',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#15191E;margin:0 0 8px;">Cause first, crack second</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">The engineer looks for what's driving the movement: a leaking pipe, a soakwell too close to the footings, tree roots, reactive clay or a rusting lintel. Patch a crack without knowing the cause and it tends to come back.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #ECE5D8;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Figtree',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#15191E;margin:0 0 8px;">Damage graded on the CSIRO scale</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">Crack widths are measured and placed in the CSIRO damage categories 0 to 4, which reproduce AS 2870. That gives you, your insurer or your builder a shared yardstick instead of 'it looks bad'.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #ECE5D8;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Figtree',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#15191E;margin:0 0 8px;">Walls, roof, floors and footings</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">Typical scope covers walls, roof structure, load-bearing floors and ceilings, and the slab and footings, looking for cracking, sagging or shifting. It's a visual assessment of the areas the engineer can get to.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #ECE5D8;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Figtree',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#15191E;margin:0 0 8px;">Recommendations in writing</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">The report sets out what the engineer thinks should happen next, which might be monitoring, fixing a water source, or design for repair work. Engineers assess and design; any building work is for a registered builder.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #ECE5D8;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Figtree',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#15191E;margin:0 0 8px;">A report you can hand on</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">An engineer's report is something you can give to an insurer, to a builder you're in dispute with, or to your own advisers when buying. Tell the engineer what it's for, because that shapes what the report needs to answer.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #ECE5D8;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Figtree',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#15191E;margin:0 0 8px;">An engineer, not a building inspector</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">Plenty of Perth 'structural inspections' are AS 4349.1 pre-purchase reports written by building inspectors. This page is about a report from a structural engineer, so ask which one you're paying for.</p>
      </div>
</div>
