<section style="padding:0 24px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto 56px;">
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 18px;">What a structural inspection in Perth covers</h2>
    <div class="rich"><p>An engineer's inspection is aimed at the parts of the house that hold it up, not the paint and the tapware. Scope varies with the problem and the house, but a crack or structural report typically looks at:</p><ul><li>external and internal walls, including double brick, render and the brickwork around windows and doors</li><li>roof structure and ceilings, for sagging or signs of load going where it shouldn't</li><li>load-bearing floors and the slab, for slope, cracking and lift</li><li>drainage, downpipes, soakwells and nearby trees, as possible causes</li><li>steel lintels over openings, where rust can crack the brickwork above</li></ul><p>It's a visual assessment of accessible areas, so if the engineer thinks something needs a closer look, such as a plumbing leak check, the report says so. Living in double brick and also thinking about opening up a wall? Mention it, because that's a separate question covered under <a href="/wall-removal-engineering/">wall removal engineering</a>.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section style="padding:0 24px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto 56px;">
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 18px;">Why Perth houses crack: sand, clay, water and rust</h2>
    <div class="rich"><p>Most of the Perth metro area sits on sand, which is normally AS 2870 Class A and moves very little with moisture. The catch is water. CSIRO notes that sandy soil is particularly easy to wash away, so a leaking pipe, a downpipe dumping roof water beside the footings, or an over-keen reticulation system can erode the ground under a wall. The City of Joondalup, for one, requires a soakwell to sit at least the width of the soakwell away from buildings so the hole doesn't undermine footings.</p><p>The eastern side is a different animal. The Guildford Formation clay outcrops over much of the eastern Perth region, and clay sites turn up particularly in the Perth Hills. Reactive clay swells when wet and shrinks when dry, which is when doors start binding and cornices crack, and tree roots can dry it out or lift footings outright. There's more on that area under <a href="/structural-engineer-perth-hills-eastern-suburbs/">Perth Hills and eastern suburbs</a>.</p><p>Near the coast, steel lintels over windows and doors can rust, and rust takes up more room than the steel it replaces. The result is cracking in the brickwork and mortar around the lintel, often a telltale line above an opening. Owners in the <a href="/structural-engineer-western-suburbs-fremantle/">western suburbs and Fremantle</a> know this one well.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section style="padding:0 24px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto 56px;">
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 18px;">Reading the report: CSIRO damage categories</h2>
    <div class="rich"><p>Engineers commonly grade crack damage using the CSIRO categories, which reproduce AS 2870-2011 Table C1. Width is the main measure, though the number of cracks counts too:</p><ul><li><strong>Category 0, negligible:</strong> under 0.1 mm, hairline cracks</li><li><strong>Category 1, very slight:</strong> under 1 mm, fine cracks</li><li><strong>Category 2, slight:</strong> under 5 mm, noticeable and easily filled; doors and windows may stick slightly</li><li><strong>Category 3, moderate:</strong> 5 to 15 mm, or several cracks of 3 mm or more in one group; doors and windows stick and pipes can fracture</li><li><strong>Category 4, severe:</strong> 15 to 25 mm, depending also on the number of cracks; frames distort and walls can lean or bulge</li></ul><p>CSIRO also warns that symptoms don't simply reflect the original cause, so a category on its own isn't a diagnosis. The engineer's job is to put the width, the pattern, the soil and the water together. A crack that's still growing is a different story from one that opened years ago and stopped, which is why the engineer may suggest monitoring before anything else.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section style="padding:0 24px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto 56px;">
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 18px;">When you need an engineer, and how it works</h2>
    <div class="rich"><p>Owners usually come to this for one of four reasons: peace of mind about a crack, an insurance claim, a dispute with a builder, or a house they're about to buy. The honest first step is finding out whether you need an engineer at all. Send photos of the cracks, a couple of wide shots of the wall and anything you know about leaks or recent work, and the engineer can say whether a site visit is warranted and quote it.</p><ul><li>Call (08) 5550 6237 or email info@kingsparkstructural.com.</li><li>We pass your details to the independent engineering firm that advertises here.</li><li>The engineer quotes you after seeing photos, plans or the site.</li><li>If you go ahead, the engineer inspects and writes the report, and you contract with them directly.</li></ul><p>If a report points to the footings, bear in mind that underpinning or replacing footings isn't exempt from a building permit in WA, so it goes through the permit process with a registered builder. On the engineering side, registration of structural engineers in WA began on 1 July 2024 with a transition period to 1 July 2027. From that date structural engineering work must be done by a registered building engineer, and you can check anyone's registration on the DEMIRS Building Engineer Register. Planning an extension rather than chasing a crack? See <a href="/extension-engineering/">extension engineering</a>.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section style="padding:72px 24px;background:#F1ECE2;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto;">
    <span class="kicker">FAQ</span>
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(24px,3.5vw,36px);margin:0 0 32px;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px;">
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #ECE5D8;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Figtree',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#15191E;margin:0 0 10px;">How wide does a crack have to be before I should worry?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">Width is only part of it, but the CSIRO categories give a yardstick: under 0.1 mm is category 0, under 1 mm category 1, under 5 mm category 2, and 5 to 15 mm, or several cracks of 3 mm or more together, category 3. CSIRO says monitoring should always be treated seriously, and a crack that keeps widening matters more than one that has stopped. Only an engineer who has seen the house can tell you what yours means.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #ECE5D8;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Figtree',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#15191E;margin:0 0 10px;">How much does a structural inspection cost in Perth?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">None of the Perth engineering firms we checked publish a fixed fee for a crack or structural report; they quote per job. The cheapest advertised figures are usually for building inspection reports, such as one Perth firm's AS 4349.1 reports from $440 incl. GST, and those are prepared by building inspectors, not engineers. As a national comparison only, Bark's 2026 figure is $560 ex GST per residential inspection and report across Australia, with no Perth figure given. More on our <a href="/structural-engineer-cost-perth/">Perth cost page</a>.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #ECE5D8;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Figtree',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#15191E;margin:0 0 10px;">Is a pre-purchase building inspection the same as a structural engineer's report?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">No. A pre-purchase report under AS 4349.1 is usually done by a building inspector, and at least one Perth inspection firm states plainly that it doesn't provide structural engineering or engineering certification. If a building report flags cracking or movement, that's usually the moment to get an engineer to look at the cause. Ask whoever you book who will actually sign the report.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #ECE5D8;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Figtree',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#15191E;margin:0 0 10px;">Will my home insurance cover cracking from ground movement?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">That depends on your policy, and nobody can promise you an outcome. Many home policies exclude gradual subsidence and ground movement, and one large WA insurer's 2021 policy wording also excluded gradual leakage over time where the owner should have known about the leak. Check your own policy's exclusions; a report on the cause is usually what an insurer will want to see.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #ECE5D8;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Figtree',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#15191E;margin:0 0 10px;">I think my builder caused the cracks. What are my options?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">Building and Energy (DEMIRS) handles building disputes in WA. A building service complaint can generally be made up to 6 years after the work was completed, and a home building work contract complaint (contracts of $7,500 to $500,000) generally within 3 years. You must serve a Notice of Proposed Complaint on the other party at least 14 days before lodging, and an independent engineer's report is often the evidence a complaint rests on.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
